# Product

<p class="lead">One design system, several apps. <strong>Llama UI</strong> draws the interface and handles interaction; each app owns its state and talks to llama-server.</p>

## The Llama stack

- **llama.cpp** runs models locally; **llama-server** is its HTTP server.
- **Llama UI** is the component library this Storybook documents.
- **Llama Chat** is the chat client built from it. It replaces the legacy web UI and ships inside llama-server.
- **Llama for Mac** and **Llama for Windows** run llama-server from the menu bar or the system tray and open Llama Chat. On a Mac it opens in a new tab of the default browser, and gets a window of its own when added to the Dock as a Safari web app.
- **llama.brand** is the one source of the icon, the fonts and the colours.

<Figure n={1} caption="The products and their repositories. llama.brand supplies the brand; llama.cpp holds the engine and Llama UI; the native apps run the engine and open Llama Chat.">
	<Products />
</Figure>

## One library, many apps

Llama UI holds UI and UX only, no business logic. Llama Chat is the first app built on it, and the llama.app website uses it too, so models look and behave the same in both. Keeping the library separate lets anyone build their own app on it.

## Llama UI draws, the apps decide

Components take data in and report what the user did. State and calls to the server belong to the app.

<Figure n={2} caption="What belongs in Llama UI and what belongs in an app. A chat app is the library's parts plus the app's own logic.">
	<Rule />
</Figure>

*Architecture › Stack* draws the whole stack and the repo layout; *Architecture › Chat* shows where each part of Llama Chat lands.

## Across the three layers

- **Application:** Llama Chat, Llama for Mac and Windows, or your own app. Ours never assume a cloud account and always show which model runs, and where. Llama UI is a library for building apps, not a layer: an app can use the layers below without it.
- **Harness**, across the line between the application and inference: the agentic loop and the tools it calls. The loop and browser tools run in the app, server tools run in llama-server, and MCP servers you add can be reached from either.
- **Inference:** llama-server, libllama and ggml, on your hardware. llama-server is run by hand, started by a native app or shared by someone else.
- **Model:** a GGUF file from Hugging Face, which the apps help find, download and keep.

<Figure n={3} caption="The Llama stack across the three layers, the harness laid across the application and inference, and the real parts on each. Code is set in Llama Mono; the dashed slot is for apps that aren't ours.">
	<StackLayers />
</Figure>

## Simple first, depth on request

Nothing comes pre-installed. On the first run, the Llama app suggests models that fit the device and recommends one: small enough for any device, and able to read, see and hear. One click downloads it and runs llama-server, with no terminal. Llama Chat opens on a conversation, not on settings. For power users, every model and server setting is there behind a disclosure, with a short explanation where a term needs one, and the model manager and Discover can be turned on in Llama Chat.

## One build, three channels

Llama Chat is a static app (HTML, JavaScript and CSS), built once per release. Today it ships inside llama-server. Planned: `npx llama-chat` and chat.llama.app, which carry the client only and connect to a server you add.

<Figure n={4} caption="The same build in three channels. Only the bundled one comes with a server.">
	<Channels />
</Figure>

The app will tell where it runs at runtime, with no build flags: if its own origin answers as llama.cpp, it runs bundled; otherwise it asks for a server. *Architecture › Channels* compares the three, with the `npx` command and the release flow.
